WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.928
This is RUNSAFE, by Marc Donovici, an IT auditor in Strasbourg.

2
00:00:05.878 --> 00:00:10.682
RUNSAFE is design software that applies mathematical proof to knitting.

3
00:00:10.682 --> 00:00:15.109
It gives knitwear a damage rating: how far one snag can run.

4
00:00:15.109 --> 00:00:19.393
So small faults stay repairable, and garments stay in use.

5
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.933
Your phone has a water rating.

6
00:00:22.883 --> 00:00:25.500
Your sunscreen has an SPF.

7
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.256
Your sweater has nothing:

8
00:00:27.256 --> 00:00:30.487
nobody can tell you how far one snag will run.

9
00:00:30.487 --> 00:00:35.760
In this plain sleeve, one dropped stitch ladders through 116 loops.

10
00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.775
In the RUNSAFE sleeve, it stops within nine,

11
00:00:38.775 --> 00:00:41.516
and that limit is mathematically proved.

12
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:49.207
Marc's day job is checking whether claims hold up.

13
00:00:49.157 --> 00:00:51.498
In fine knitwear, a snag at the elbow

14
00:00:51.498 --> 00:00:54.091
of a plain sleeve can ladder to the cuff,

15
00:00:54.091 --> 00:00:57.089
and an almost intact top can end up thrown away.

16
00:00:57.089 --> 00:00:58.970
RUNSAFE began as a question:

17
00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:02.935
could knitwear carry a damage rating that anyone can check?

18
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.711
The mechanism is simple.

19
00:01:07.661 --> 00:01:10.141
Every eight rows comes a barrier course,

20
00:01:10.141 --> 00:01:13.365
where each loop is held by two loops instead of one.

21
00:01:13.365 --> 00:01:16.733
When a ladder reaches it, the second holder stops it,

22
00:01:16.733 --> 00:01:19.276
and the proof doesn't count on friction.

23
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:27.790
The hard part is proving it.

24
00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:30.423
Up to nine stitches can drop from this

25
00:01:30.423 --> 00:01:33.247
one sleeve in a 33-digit number of ways.

26
00:01:33.247 --> 00:01:34.977
No lab can test them all,

27
00:01:34.977 --> 00:01:37.883
so the worst case is proved for every one.

28
00:01:37.883 --> 00:01:40.719
First, the fabric: at most k times

29
00:01:40.719 --> 00:01:44.641
k-plus-one over two segments open, at any size.

30
00:01:44.641 --> 00:01:47.561
Second, the machine: compiling adds loops,

31
00:01:47.561 --> 00:01:52.219
and the bound is proved to survive, faults in those loops included.

32
00:01:52.219 --> 00:01:59.185
Third, the cost: from 155 times the machine passes, down to 1.31.

33
00:01:59.185 --> 00:02:06.332
And every result is checked with Carnegie Mellon's open-source knitting simulator,

34
00:02:06.332 --> 00:02:08.859
and 859,689 exhaustive tests.

35
00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:13.701
So a designer no longer draws barriers.

36
00:02:13.651 --> 00:02:17.749
They state the rating: one snag within a centimetre,

37
00:02:17.749 --> 00:02:22.557
nine snags under 450 loops, at most 35 percent more knitting.

38
00:02:22.557 --> 00:02:26.284
RUNSAFE checks the proved constructions in its library,

39
00:02:26.284 --> 00:02:29.875
and returns the cheapest: a barrier every eight rows.

40
00:02:29.875 --> 00:02:33.846
Ask for 300 loops, and nothing fits the budget.

41
00:02:33.846 --> 00:02:37.717
Allow 50 percent, and it switches to staggered barriers,

42
00:02:37.717 --> 00:02:42.141
compiled and verified, with a Shima Seiki machine file exported.

43
00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:53.115
The rating goes on the care label.

44
00:02:53.065 --> 00:02:56.914
Scan it, and your own phone downloads the knitting program,

45
00:02:56.914 --> 00:02:59.784
rebuilds its loops, and re-checks the proof.

46
00:02:59.784 --> 00:03:02.224
A brand, a repairer or a buyer can

47
00:03:02.224 --> 00:03:05.526
verify it without taking anyone's word for it.

48
00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:14.765
If a snag happens, the Studio shows which loops were released,

49
00:03:14.765 --> 00:03:16.579
and lists those to re-form.

50
00:03:16.529 --> 00:03:20.904
How repairers restore barrier loops is one of the first tests.

51
00:03:20.904 --> 00:03:23.884
The sleeve also stays one continuous yarn,

52
00:03:23.884 --> 00:03:26.581
with a deliberate route to unravel it.

53
00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.737
So, where does this go?

54
00:03:32.688 --> 00:03:35.976
Fine knitwear first, where quality failures such as

55
00:03:35.976 --> 00:03:39.328
holes account for about a third of disposal reasons.

56
00:03:39.328 --> 00:03:45.839
Next, tights, the classic ladder: 130 million pairs a year in France alone.

57
00:03:45.839 --> 00:03:49.979
Then, a damage rating in digital product passports.

58
00:03:49.979 --> 00:03:53.246
And the climate case: in a worked wool example,

59
00:03:53.246 --> 00:03:58.599
the barrier repays its extra knitting if it prevents 0.7 percent of discards.

60
00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:06.422
The three biggest challenges are physical validation, practical repair, and adoption.

61
00:04:06.372 --> 00:04:08.420
First, a knitting laboratory,

62
00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:13.221
to test the predicted damage limit after knitting, washing and wear.

63
00:04:13.221 --> 00:04:18.656
Second, repair specialists, to establish how barrier loops can be restored,

64
00:04:18.656 --> 00:04:19.888
and at what cost.

65
00:04:19.888 --> 00:04:25.956
Third, a brand partner, to judge appearance, production time and willingness to pay.

66
00:04:25.956 --> 00:04:30.227
That is the next step: turning a checked design into knitwear.
